# Git hooks are run by sh. A CRLF line ending after the `#!/bin/sh` shebang makes the
# kernel look for an interpreter literally named "/bin/sh\r", and the failure surfaces as
# a confusing "bad interpreter: no such file or directory" or, worse on Windows, as the
# hook silently not running - which would leave you believing you are protected when you
# are not. Force LF regardless of anyone's core.autocrlf setting.
.githooks/* text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf

# PowerShell is fine with either, but keeping the repo consistent means a diff never
# shows a whole file changed because someone's editor rewrote the endings.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

*.cpp       text
*.h         text
*.c         text
*.md        text
*.def       text

# The files a user actually unzips and opens. These leave the development environment
# entirely, so they get CRLF explicitly rather than whatever the checkout happened to
# produce - the release zip copies them straight out of the working tree, and the reader
# on the other end is on Windows in an unknown editor.
*.txt          text eol=crlf
LICENSE        text eol=crlf
*.ini.example  text eol=crlf
